<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albrecht von Wallenstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1583">1583</a>–<a href="/years/1634">1634</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Albrecht von Waldstein</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albrecht von Wallenstein was a Bohemian nobleman who raised and commanded the imperial armies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. Rewarded with the duchies of Friedland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagan">Sagan</a> and, in 1628, Mecklenburg, he became one of the most powerful men in the Empire. Suspected of treason, he was dismissed and then murdered at Eger in 1634 on the emperor's authority.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein.jpg" alt="Albrecht von Wallenstein" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julius Schnorr von Carolsfeld · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albrecht_von_Wallenstein.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Albrecht von Wallenstein was born on 24 September 1583 at Heřmanice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, into a poor Protestant branch of the old Czech House of Waldstein. His parents were Lutheran and Utraquist Hussite, and after their deaths he lived for two years with his uncle Heinrich Slavata, a member of the Unity of the Brethren, whose faith he adopted. He studied at the Protestant Latin school at Goldberg and then at the University of Altdorf, where brawls and sword fights landed him in the town prison. He later studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> and became fluent in German, Czech, Latin and Italian.</p><p>He served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-turkish-war">Long Turkish War</a> from 1604 to 1606, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 1606, and in 1609 married a wealthy widow whose estates came to him when she died in 1614. Three years later he began a career as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> by raising forces for the Emperor in the Uskok War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the Bohemian Revolt of 1618 he took the Catholic side, marching his regiment to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> with the Moravian treasury. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> on 8 November 1620 he secured confiscated Protestant lands and formed them into the Duchy of Friedland in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. He became an imperial count palatine in 1622, a prince in 1623 and Duke of Friedland in 1625. That year he raised an army of 50,000 men, and a year later he defeated the Protestants at Dessau Bridge.</p><p>He became imperial generalissimo and, from April 1628, Admiral of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. In 1630 Emperor Ferdinand II, wary of his ambition, released him from service. Protestant victories led Ferdinand to recall him, and he defeated the Swedish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> at Alte Veste; Gustavus was later killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a>. He relied on astrologers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a>, who drew a horoscope for him, and he suffered from painful joint illness from 1620.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the summer of 1633 Wallenstein arranged armistices to negotiate peace, believing the war could last decades. Plotters accused him of treachery, and a secret court found him guilty. On 24 January 1634 the Emperor signed a secret patent removing him from command, and an open patent of 18 February charged him with high treason. Losing the support of his army, he travelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plzen">Plzeň</a> to Cheb on 23 February hoping to meet the Swedes.</p><p>On the night of 25 February Scottish and Irish officers in his force killed his loyal officers at a feast and then Wallenstein himself in his lodgings. The Emperor rewarded the assassins. Plays, poems and pamphlets about him appeared soon afterwards, and Schiller made his last period the subject of a play trilogy completed in 1788.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albrecht von Wallenstein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The duchy’s weakness was exposed in the Thirty Years’ War: in 1627 Bogislaw XIV had to accept imperial troops under <b>Albrecht von Wallenstein</b>, who besieged Stralsund in 1628 and failed to take it after the town received Danish and Swedish help.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Thirty Years’ War the Bohemian revolt was crushed at the Battle of White Mountain on 8 November 1620, and imperial armies raised by <b>Albrecht von Wallenstein</b> fought across Germany until the Peace of Westphalia in 1648.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Thirty Years' War of 1618 to 1648 reduced imperial military pretensions almost to nothing, as Swedish, French and Spanish armies operated across German territory while imperial forces relied on Habsburg dynastic troops and contractors such as <b>Albrecht von Wallenstein</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At Breitenfeld (1631), Swedish and Saxon forces routed Tilly's veteran Catholic League army, and at Lützen (1632) the Swedes held the field against <b>Wallenstein</b> even though Gustavus himself was killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A Swedish army under King Gustavus Adolphus, supported by troops from Saxony and Hesse-Kassel, narrowly defeated an imperial force under <b>Albrecht von Wallenstein</b> at Lützen.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-16">16 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Thirty Years' War <b>Wallenstein</b> held Mecklenburg from 1628 to 1631.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg">Duchy of Mecklenburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Heřmanice, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in Cheb, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.38,15.92&amp;year=1616" aria-label="Heřmanice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4914 413 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Albrecht von Wallenstein"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5364 683h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5364 683h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.38,15.92&amp;year=1616">What was here in 1616? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
